{
    "manifestVersion": 1,
    "_comment_public": "true — Q-042 is now resolved on both halves: publisher ('GeekIt') and listing (public, decided by the user 2026-08-07). Public discoverability under D11 (buyers the vendor has never met) is the reason chosen over per-organisation sharing's distribution control. This DOES trigger Microsoft's public-listing qualifications (own/develop/licensed to distribute, actively maintained) and may prompt a demo and review — a consequence to expect at first publish, not something this manifest can pre-empt. A-13's cost of the private route (no auto-update; that customer owns their own version, and skew can run in either direction, AC-F-009-6) no longer applies to this, the production listing — it is exactly why the private `gcode-for-devops-dev` counterpart (extension/vss-extension.dev-overrides.json) exists instead, for development and testing only.",
    "_comment_gallery_flags": "'Paid': this is a paid listing — GeekIt (the publisher) owns billing and licensing directly (per-organisation flat annual, invoiced), because Microsoft provides neither commerce nor entitlement enforcement for Azure DevOps extensions on its own. 'Paid' is a ONE-WAY DOOR: Microsoft's own documentation states an extension marked free can't be changed to paid, and by the same asymmetry once marked Paid it is not walked back either — this is the same class of irreversible decision as the publisher id above, and just as deliberate. 'Preview' is a maturity badge only (no time limit, no gating, and unlike 'Paid' it IS reversible) — signals early-adopter expectations honestly rather than presenting a first release as fully mature. Neither flag implies or requires galleryproperties.trialDays — deliberately absent: under BYOL there is no Marketplace-run trial to advertise. ~~and this product has no licence key, activation call or entitlement check of any kind (Q-036, resolved)~~ SUPERSEDED 2026-08-16 — Q-036 was re-answered and the product now enforces a licence (F-011-F-014, NFR-022, approved); struck rather than deleted because implementer and this repository's other agents read this file as binding. The half that survives, and is load-bearing: this is not Marketplace licensing coming into play — still no Marketplace-run trial, no Marketplace commerce, no per-seat Marketplace entitlement check, which is exactly why 'Paid' still needs the licensing override below and __BYOLENFORCED still applies. The licence itself is enforced by the proxy, in the customer's own tenant, with no vendor endpoint in any runtime path (NFR-022.1) — see stack.md §10 for the full correction this mirrors. Applies to the production listing only — the private dev counterpart never carries 'Paid' (extension/vss-extension.dev-overrides.json suppresses this key entirely; build/manifest-targets.mjs's ALLOWED_DEV_OVERRIDE_KEYS is where that is enforced, not left to convention).",
    "_comment_tags": "'__BYOLENFORCED' records, for Marketplace's own tooling, that licensing is enforced off-platform (bring-your-own-license — GeekIt's invoicing, not a Marketplace-billed seat) rather than through Marketplace's built-in per-seat commerce. '__DoNotDownload' removes the Download option from the listing so it is a shop window (install via the Marketplace/organisation flow) rather than a distribution point for a standalone .vsix. Production-listing-only, same as galleryFlags above — extension/vss-extension.dev-overrides.json suppresses this key for the private dev counterpart.",
    "_comment_links": "Required alongside 'Paid' above: tfx-cli refuses to package a Paid extension with no support link (ADR-023 §'What is actually true today', point 4). The URL is the user's to choose, not this repository's to invent (ADR-023 Decision 1's closing note) — supplied 2026-08-08, confirmed live and both mailboxes (support@, security@) tested by the site owner 2026-08-09 (T-091's unblock evidence). Production-listing-only in effect (a Paid requirement), but left in the shared base rather than the dev-overrides suppression list for the same reason 'licensing' above is: an inert extra key on a non-paid, private listing has no effect for Marketplace to act on.",
    "_comment_two_extension_pattern": "This is the ONE base manifest, per Microsoft's recommended two-extension pattern: 'maintain two extensions: publisher.extension, public in the Marketplace ... and publisher.extension-dev, private, shared only with your organization ... You don't need two copies of your source code—just maintain separate manifest files.' The private dev counterpart ('GeekIt.gcode-for-devops-dev') is produced from this exact file by build/package-vsix.mjs, which merges extension/vss-extension.dev-overrides.json onto it in memory (build/manifest-targets.mjs's computeEffectiveManifest, replace semantics) and writes the complete result to a generated, git-ignored file that tfx packages as-is (ADR-023 Decision 1) — never tfx's own overrides-merging flag, which concatenates arrays instead of replacing them and cannot be trusted to suppress anything (SEC-019). build/manifest-targets.mjs names the only fields allowed to differ (id, name, public/sharing, galleryFlags, tags — the last two added so the dev overrides file can positively suppress 'Paid'/'Preview' and '__BYOLENFORCED'/'__DoNotDownload' rather than silently inherit them; a private, unlisted extension carries none of the four) and build/package-vsix.mjs's dev-manifest-drift guard fails packaging if the overrides file ever asks for more. Scopes, contributions and the licensing override are therefore identical between the two by construction, not by convention.",
    "_comment_publisher": "'GeekIt' is the real publisher ID, created in the Visual Studio Marketplace publishing portal on 2026-08-07 and confirmed by the user from the portal itself (Q-042, resolved). It must match the portal exactly, and this is effectively permanent: per Microsoft's publishing documentation an extension can only be unpublished if it is free, and only removed outright while it has ZERO installs — so once any customer installs, publisher.id is the extension's identity forever. That is what AC-F-008-1 protects. Do not change it, and do not let a second publisher appear for the same product. build/package-vsix.mjs's placeholder guard stays in place: it is now dormant rather than obsolete, and it is what would catch a future edit that reintroduced a stand-in value.",
    "_comment_version": "'0.0.0' is a fixed sentinel, never a real release number. The one canonical version source is the repo-root VERSION file; build/package-vsix.mjs reads it and passes it to 'tfx extension create --override' at packaging time, so this literal is never what actually ships. If this literal is ever hand-edited away from '0.0.0', it becomes a second, independently-typed version source — build/package-vsix.mjs's checks then require it to match VERSION exactly, or refuse to package.",
    "_comment_scopes": "Every scope below is requested because a specific acceptance criterion needs it — one line each, with the AC, in build/scope-justification.md. ci/check-manifest-scopes.mjs (run by build/package-vsix.mjs, and independently in CI per T-046) fails if a scope here has no line there, or if a line there has no scope here. No scope is requested speculatively (BR-F-008-1, AC-F-008-2).",
    "scopes": [
        "vso.work_write",
        "vso.code"
    ],
    "_comment_contribution": "ADR-017 decided the work-item-form entry points: the work-item-form-page tab, id 'work-item-panel' (load-bearing: extension/src/kernel/ado/host.ts's isWorkItemPanelContribution matches contribution ids ending '.work-item-panel', and ADO composes the runtime id as '<publisher>.<id>.<contribution id>' — so this field, not any other, is what makes extension/src/app/contribution.tsx's dispatch match anything at all). The ms.vss-work-web.work-item-toolbar-menu action ADR-017 withdrew is not declared, and never will be without a task that reopens that decision. ADR-039 (2026-08-13) adds a second, different-typed contribution: 'settings-hub' (ms.vss-web.hub, targeting ms.vss-web.collection-admin-hub-group) — the organisation-settings surface where an admin enters the proxy's base URL, per AC-F-007-5's install handover. Its own id is load-bearing in the same way: fixed, approved, permanent, never renamed once a customer has installed (ADR-039). T-150 declares this contribution and widens the guards below to match; T-151 builds the screen its 'uri' points at — until then this contribution ships inert (ADR-019 rule 10), which is correct and stated, not an oversight. The invariant is now exactly two contributions, one of each type, and nothing else (ADR-039 'Consequences'). extension/vss-extension.test.ts asserts both — the work-item-panel suffix coupling by reading host.ts's real constant, not a copy of the string — and build/package-vsix.mjs re-asserts the same shape before it will package.",
    "contributions": [
        {
            "id": "work-item-panel",
            "type": "ms.vss-work-web.work-item-form-page",
            "description": "G-Code — a work item chat panel with subject context, field-edit and decomposition proposals, and curated skills.",
            "targets": [
                "ms.vss-work-web.work-item-form"
            ],
            "properties": {
                "name": "AI Assistant",
                "uri": "dist/index.html"
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "settings-hub",
            "type": "ms.vss-web.hub",
            "targets": [
                "ms.vss-web.collection-admin-hub-group"
            ],
            "properties": {
                "name": "G-Code",
                "uri": "dist/settings-hub.html"
            }
        }
    ],
    "_comment_licensing": "Required alongside 'Paid' above: Microsoft's paid-extension manifest reference documents that Marketplace hides EVERY contribution behind its own per-seat licensing check by default once an extension is marked 'Paid' — a check this product can never satisfy, because no seat is ever purchased through Marketplace commerce (BYOL: GeekIt invoices directly). ~~and Q-036 already ruled out any licence key, activation call or entitlement check of the product's own~~ SUPERSEDED 2026-08-16 — Q-036 was re-answered and the product now enforces a licence (F-011-F-014, NFR-022, approved), struck rather than deleted for the same reason as the gallery-flags comment above. The half that survives: 'AlwaysInclude' is still exactly right and MUST NOT change — it exists to defeat MARKETPLACE'S OWN per-seat gate (which BYOL genuinely never satisfies and never will, licence or no licence), not to describe whether the product enforces anything of its own. The product's own licence enforcement is unrelated to this override, runs entirely inside the proxy in the customer's tenant, and involves no Marketplace commerce, no per-seat entitlement and no vendor endpoint (NFR-022.1) — see stack.md §10. Without this override, Marketplace's default gate would hide the contribution it applies to from every customer who installs, which is worse than not listing the extension at all — one entry per contribution, so ADR-039's second contribution ('settings-hub') needs its own line alongside 'work-item-panel''s. 'AlwaysInclude' is the one behavior value that keeps a contribution visible with no Marketplace-side entitlement to check. build/package-vsix.mjs's licensing-override guard iterates the manifest's own contributions array (never a copied id or count) and re-asserts this per contribution before it will package a 'Paid' target — so it already catches a missing entry here with no code change, only this data addition. The private dev counterpart never carries 'Paid', so this section is inert for it either way; it is left in the shared base rather than added to the dev-overrides suppression list because an inert extra key on a non-paid listing has no effect for Marketplace to act on.",
    "licensing": {
        "overrides": [
            {
                "id": "work-item-panel",
                "behavior": "AlwaysInclude"
            },
            {
                "id": "settings-hub",
                "behavior": "AlwaysInclude"
            }
        ]
    },
    "_comment_content": "T-045 wrote the listing text (marketplace-listing-content.md, repo root) but never wired it anywhere — vss-extension.json had no 'content' block, so the Marketplace 'Overview' tab (which Azure DevOps renders from 'content.details', a markdown file packaged inside the VSIX) had nothing to render and shipped empty for GeekIt.gcode-for-devops-dev 1.0.0. 'extension/overview.md' is the shippable copy: marketplace-listing-content.md's own header names itself unshippable as-is ('when the actual Marketplace listing page is composed ... delete this comment block and the Editorial notes appendix, which are for the person assembling the listing, not for a buyer') and instructs an assembler to extract the buyer-facing sections in AC-F-008-4's load-bearing order. overview.md is that extraction: the leading audit-trail comment, the 'Self-check against NFR-018.6' checklist and the 'Placeholders'/'Editorial notes' appendices are assembler-only apparatus (task ids, unresolved-AC caveats, sourcing citations) and are excluded on the same 'not for a buyer' principle the source file states for the two appendices it names outright — none of it is a fact a buyer needs, and the Self-check's one open item (AC-F-012-1's licence-reload gap) is not something to surface on a live listing. The one named gap that could not be resolved (a screenshot/demo asset for the gallery, `AC-F-008-15`'s Placeholders section) was left out rather than invented and is reported separately, not guessed at here. The overview is a PACKAGED FILE, not portal-entered text, because this repository's whole discipline is that every fact reaching a buyer is sourced from an approved document and checked into version control alongside the code that makes it true (NFR-018.6, AC-F-008-12) — text typed once into the publisher portal is unreviewable, unversioned and invisible to every guard below; a file in 'files' is diffed, reviewed and packaged the same way `dist` and the icons are.",
    "_comment_icons": "T-371 (ADR-074): the manifest's first 'icons' block. 'default' (Marketplace convention: 128x128 PNG) is the listing tile; the dark tile was chosen deliberately because the Marketplace page background is light (docs/02-ux/icon/gcode-tile-dark.svg is the rasterised source — the light tile and both '-outlined' variants in that folder were rejected by the owner and must not be substituted in). 'large' (512x512 PNG) is the branding/details-page icon — confirmed a real, tfx-recognised key (vsix-manifest-builder.js's 'icons' case maps every key generically to a 'Microsoft.VisualStudio.Services.Icons.<StartCase(key)>' asset type, so 'large' composes to the documented 'Icons.Large' asset exactly as 'default' composes to 'Icons.Default'), not guessed. Both PNGs are also declared in 'files' below (belt-and-suspenders with build/package-vsix.mjs's guard, which reads this manifest's own JSON rather than trusting tfx's internal auto-file registration) even though tfx would auto-register them from this block alone.",
    "_comment_files": "'dist' is exactly what 'npm run build' (tsc -b && vite build) emits (extension/dist/index.html plus its hashed extension/dist/assets/*) — packaged whole so the contribution's 'dist/index.html' uri and every asset it references resolve inside the VSIX without this manifest tracking individual hashed filenames. 'images/icon-128.png' and 'images/icon-512.png' are the icons.default/icons.large assets above (T-371) — ux-designer's exported PNGs, rasterised from the dark tile. 'overview.md' is the 'content.details' asset above — tfx-cli would auto-register it from that block alone (vsix-manifest-builder.js's 'details'/'content' cases both set 'auto: true'), but this entry is declared explicitly anyway, belt-and-suspenders with the icons above, because build/manifest-targets.mjs's shape guard reads this manifest's own JSON rather than trusting tfx's internal auto-file registration.",
    "contributionTypes": []
}